VALUES ('dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 'ccs-practice-4', 'CCS Practice Paper 4', 'ccs', ARRAY['ICD-10-CM', 'ICD-10-PCS', 'Obstetric Coding', 'Behavioral Health Coding', 'Substance Use Disorders', 'Injury Coding', 'Anti-Kickback/Stark Basics', 'Provider Queries', 'Interoperability']::TEXT[], 100, 'advanced', true, 4, 240);

-- ── Section: Domain 1 — Coding Knowledge and Skills (40 questions) — Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank. ──

VALUES ('6e179e6c-f2e3-44b2-bda2-9a72f8c7df04', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 1, 'In ICD-10-CM obstetric coding, the principal diagnosis for a routine, uncomplicated vaginal delivery of a single liveborn infant would generally include a code reflecting:');
VALUES ('6e179e6c-f2e3-44b2-bda2-9a72f8c7df04', '58698167-f53c-4dfd-a2f5-063f83155382', 'ICD-10-CM obstetric guidelines direct that an outcome-of-delivery code be assigned on the mother''s record for every delivery — a frequently overlooked but guideline-mandated element even for routine, uncomplicated deliveries.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('26b0168a-bede-4427-b0b7-bbff38e66b5c', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 2, 'Which of the following best describes the ICD-10-CM ''trimester'' character convention used in many obstetric codes (Chapter 15)?');
VALUES ('26b0168a-bede-4427-b0b7-bbff38e66b5c', '9463f4bb-9a17-4383-b5bc-7cd55d804eaf', 'Obstetric codes frequently require a trimester designation reflecting the CURRENT status at the time of the encounter (which can differ from the trimester when a condition originally arose) — a genuinely nuanced, frequently tested obstetric coding convention.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('34185817-e72e-418a-857f-6c6ebfc862b8', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 3, 'A pregnant patient is admitted with preeclampsia and subsequently delivers via cesarean section due to the preeclampsia. Correct sequencing generally requires:');
VALUES ('34185817-e72e-418a-857f-6c6ebfc862b8', 'cf6c2372-5597-4410-b844-46b2c433a3fd', 'Chapter 15 (obstetric) guidelines generally prioritize the complication of pregnancy as principal diagnosis when it is the reason for the admission/procedure — a foundational obstetric sequencing principle, alongside the outcome-of-delivery and procedure coding already discussed.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('5eb2a33c-98af-4743-b6d3-43db5b1b1718', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 4, 'For a newborn''s own health record (separate from the mother''s), the principal diagnosis for an otherwise healthy, term newborn delivered without complications is typically:');
VALUES ('5eb2a33c-98af-4743-b6d3-43db5b1b1718', '34ccca79-3c6f-4a50-9639-5caec69a6013', 'The newborn''s own record uses a liveborn-infant code (by place of birth and single/multiple gestation) as principal diagnosis for a routine, uncomplicated birth — distinct from and not duplicating the mother''s own obstetric principal diagnosis.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('a5d59ca6-787c-4272-b994-ed3648ff4e4d', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 5, 'A newborn is documented as being born at 34 weeks gestation. Which of the following best reflects correct ICD-10-CM coding for this newborn''s gestational age?');
VALUES ('a5d59ca6-787c-4272-b994-ed3648ff4e4d', '863d954f-b79b-4e51-99fa-39ce592ba8e8', 'ICD-10-CM includes specific gestational-age codes for preterm/extreme immaturity, which should be assigned in addition to other newborn diagnoses when documented — an important newborn-coding-specificity element, not limited to only NICU cases.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('7a003a82-1af4-4eae-bdb9-eead6862f5a8', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 6, 'Which of the following best describes ICD-10-CM''s approach to coding a substance use disorder (e.g., alcohol use disorder) together with an associated intoxication or withdrawal state?');
VALUES ('7a003a82-1af4-4eae-bdb9-eead6862f5a8', '53994656-2e65-4ec7-bcc4-0fd847b3dfe8', 'ICD-10-CM''s substance-related disorder codes are structured with combination options capturing the use disorder AND associated intoxication/withdrawal/induced conditions — mirroring the same combination-code design principle seen elsewhere (diabetes, heart failure, sickle cell).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('f26bd671-6bb5-4cc8-8b48-c4440c3a1fa1', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 7, 'Which of the following best distinguishes ''substance abuse'' from ''substance dependence'' as historically used terms, in relation to current ICD-10-CM classification of substance use disorders?');
VALUES ('f26bd671-6bb5-4cc8-8b48-c4440c3a1fa1', '85d406d7-e75c-4d43-a0cb-28e27a3b98f8', 'This tests awareness that ICD-10-CM''s substance use disorder classification structure (while retaining ''abuse'' terminology in some code descriptors) generally reflects a severity-spectrum concept closer to DSM-5, rather than a strict carryover of the older DSM-IV abuse/dependence dichotomy — a nuance worth recognizing rather than assuming exact DSM-IV terminology parity.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('c9c152c9-39af-4039-8b44-8632d73085de', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 8, 'A patient with documented major depressive disorder, recurrent, currently in partial remission, should be coded using:');
VALUES ('c9c152c9-39af-4039-8b44-8632d73085de', 'bad21fe4-a945-4a71-ab10-e997b9840c66', 'ICD-10-CM''s depressive disorder codes capture episode pattern (single/recurrent), severity, and remission status when documented — coding to a generic unspecified code when this specificity is actually documented under-codes the encounter, the same specificity principle applied throughout Domain 1.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('b0c7d0e1-fc1b-4248-9a38-1cb3943dc4aa', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 9, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-CM coding when a patient has both a documented psychiatric diagnosis (e.g., schizophrenia) and a co-occurring substance use disorder, both actively addressed during the same admission?');
VALUES ('b0c7d0e1-fc1b-4248-9a38-1cb3943dc4aa', 'efb17e2f-a52e-4995-bab1-f074a25812e8', 'Co-occurring psychiatric and substance use disorder diagnoses are each coded per their own specific codes/guidelines when both are clinically addressed — there''s no ICD-10-CM rule limiting behavioral health encounters to a single diagnosis, and no generic ''dual diagnosis'' combination code exists for this purpose.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('f555b57c-4079-4ab6-bfbc-6caa2efd98ea', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 10, 'Which of the following best describes the ICD-10-CM 7th character extension convention as applied to a traumatic injury code for a ''subsequent encounter, with delayed healing''?');
VALUES ('f555b57c-4079-4ab6-bfbc-6caa2efd98ea', '35597508-b3a8-4e60-b974-b9636a6669b2', 'Certain injury/fracture code categories provide distinct subsequent-encounter 7th character options reflecting routine healing versus delayed healing/nonunion/malunion — a specificity nuance within the broader 7th-character convention already covered generally.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('18184aa3-fac0-42fa-b6f7-38112f5ec525', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 11, 'Which of the following best describes coding a fracture with documented ''nonunion'' at a subsequent encounter?');
VALUES ('18184aa3-fac0-42fa-b6f7-38112f5ec525', '2c72eede-02b0-4f9b-b0c3-c4a13a0062a4', 'This extends the delayed-healing/subsequent-encounter concept to nonunion specifically — many fracture code categories provide distinct 7th character options for routine healing, delayed healing, nonunion, and malunion at subsequent encounters, each reflecting a clinically distinct healing status.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('de4123e9-e142-481d-a045-e79fd846552a', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 12, 'A patient sustains multiple traumatic injuries in a motor vehicle collision (e.g., a femur fracture, a splenic laceration, and a closed head injury). Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-CM principal diagnosis selection among multiple significant traumatic injuries?');
VALUES ('de4123e9-e142-481d-a045-e79fd846552a', 'e9ccbfe9-77ec-486d-8288-7ed32a255290', 'Multi-trauma principal diagnosis selection still follows the general UHDDS framework — while the most severe injury is OFTEN the reason for admission in practice, this isn''t an automatic/fixed rule; the actual clinical circumstances of the specific case should govern, and all clinically significant injuries meeting the additional-diagnosis criteria are coded.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('b7178cb2-cff2-462f-95e6-516e34270ffc', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 13, 'Which of the following best describes the ICD-10-CM convention for coding multiple fractures of the same bone versus fractures of different bones sustained in the same traumatic event?');
VALUES ('b7178cb2-cff2-462f-95e6-516e34270ffc', '04514e0d-96a6-4124-a7ea-1b2d8da9532a', 'Multiple-fracture coding genuinely varies by the specific bone/code category''s own structure and conventions — recognizing this as an area requiring code-specific verification (not a single universal rule) reflects accurate, careful coding practice rather than an oversimplified assumption.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('c6d73230-7c31-4f73-b9d2-3f0609dfa414', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 14, 'Which of the following best describes an ''open'' versus ''closed'' fracture designation and its relevance to ICD-10-CM code selection?');
VALUES ('c6d73230-7c31-4f73-b9d2-3f0609dfa414', 'e21dbb22-8a97-41b0-ac04-a43f9cc19c8f', 'Open/closed status is frequently a direct code-selection element (not just a severity descriptor) within ICD-10-CM''s fracture code structure, and for certain fracture sites even the specific open-fracture classification type affects code choice — a documentation detail with real coding-accuracy consequences.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('42f2d974-3d63-4e9d-ae3d-bfacf5c8e626', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 15, 'A patient''s chart documents an injury as being caused by ''assault, physical altercation.'' Which of the following best describes the coding relevance of external cause coding here?');
VALUES ('42f2d974-3d63-4e9d-ae3d-bfacf5c8e626', 'cd3f16c0-cf8f-4e51-969f-5e105dc123d7', 'External cause codes specifically capture intent (including assault) as supplemental data reported alongside the actual injury code — reinforcing the earlier general external-cause-coding principle (never principal/first-listed, always supplemental) with an intent-specific example.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('057b514e-2778-4bfd-a214-475cc84c850f', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 16, 'Which of the following best describes correct sequencing/coding when a patient''s injury is the result of a documented suicide attempt (self-harm)?');
VALUES ('057b514e-2778-4bfd-a214-475cc84c850f', 'cfb5cb99-0d7b-473a-b6a4-4a57042a072a', 'This applies the general injury/external-cause-coding framework to a self-harm scenario specifically — the physical injury is coded per standard injury coding principles, with the self-harm intent captured via the appropriate external cause code, and any addressed psychiatric diagnosis coded separately per its own criteria.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('b637e117-e3d7-4cd4-ac42-fe1374e55f89', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 17, 'Which of the following best describes the general ICD-10-CM guideline regarding coding ''adverse effect'' versus ''poisoning'' when a patient has a reaction to a medication?');
VALUES ('b637e117-e3d7-4cd4-ac42-fe1374e55f89', '0040f6d7-b78b-4616-a6df-2ca8c4b36fba', 'The adverse-effect-versus-poisoning distinction hinges on whether the drug was correctly prescribed/administered (adverse effect) versus incorrectly used in some way (poisoning) — a foundational, frequently tested distinction with different sequencing/coding conventions for each scenario.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('50c27731-b322-4d1e-b93a-453996a1f01d', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 18, 'Which of the following best describes correct sequencing for a documented poisoning case (e.g., accidental overdose of a prescribed opioid)?');
VALUES ('50c27731-b322-4d1e-b93a-453996a1f01d', '5cb2cf32-0d39-4c53-be50-c6549df90e91', 'Poisoning coding sequences the poisoning code first, followed by manifestation code(s), with an external cause code capturing intent — the reverse emphasis from adverse effect coding, where the condition/manifestation is typically sequenced first followed by the adverse-effect-of-drug code.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('d608c2eb-85bf-47ab-960b-9a9bb90d0592', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 19, 'Which of the following best describes the correct approach to coding an adverse effect scenario (properly prescribed and administered medication causing a reaction, e.g., a rash from a properly prescribed antibiotic)?');
VALUES ('d608c2eb-85bf-47ab-960b-9a9bb90d0592', '2fb8fb1e-b934-42e9-b7dc-653b17bc086c', 'This is the mirror-image sequencing convention from poisoning: for an adverse effect (correct drug use, unexpected reaction), the manifestation is generally sequenced first, followed by the adverse-effect-of-drug code — the opposite emphasis from poisoning''s drug-code-first sequencing.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('998e25b0-30cb-4dce-8306-1b458d7f44b0', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 20, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-PCS coding for an electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) procedure administered for treatment-resistant major depression?');
VALUES ('998e25b0-30cb-4dce-8306-1b458d7f44b0', '25bbf81a-a4c2-4ea8-9ec2-9a64106ae95a', 'ICD-10-PCS includes a distinct Mental Health section (and related structure) with its own codes for services like ECT, individual/group psychotherapy performed in certain inpatient contexts, and similar behavioral-health-specific procedures — a structurally separate part of ICD-10-PCS from the Medical and Surgical section''s root operations.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('7858e061-fed9-4df4-906f-1c2e18f41f1a', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 21, 'A pregnant patient with pre-existing type 1 diabetes (diagnosed years before pregnancy) is coded during her prenatal visits using which category of ICD-10-CM code?');
VALUES ('7858e061-fed9-4df4-906f-1c2e18f41f1a', 'a6cc02af-f429-40ae-86ea-86eb9966227e', 'ICD-10-CM distinguishes pre-existing (pregestational) diabetes complicating pregnancy from gestational diabetes (which develops during pregnancy) — using the gestational diabetes code for a patient with known pre-existing diabetes would misrepresent the clinical history.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('e06d23a8-e65c-4488-9d0f-12fd97233243', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 22, 'A patient at 32 weeks gestation is admitted for preterm labor that is successfully managed and does not result in delivery during this admission. The correct principal diagnosis is most likely:');
VALUES ('e06d23a8-e65c-4488-9d0f-12fd97233243', 'ae5a68df-d0a4-4ed8-b062-241fc41da9db', 'When preterm labor is managed without resulting in delivery during that specific admission, the principal diagnosis reflects the actual reason for THIS encounter (preterm labor without delivery) — the outcome-of-delivery code is used only for the encounter during which delivery actually occurs.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('80420a65-017b-4551-8ff1-616de2919495', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 23, 'Which of the following best describes the correct use of ICD-10-CM''s ''supervision of pregnancy'' Z codes (from a category commonly used for routine prenatal care)?');
VALUES ('80420a65-017b-4551-8ff1-616de2919495', '77b1434b-97bf-4f62-a450-eaa613c1fe14', 'Supervision-of-pregnancy Z codes are for routine, uncomplicated prenatal care encounters — once an actual complication becomes the focus of a given encounter, the specific complication code (not the routine supervision Z code) becomes the appropriate diagnosis for that encounter.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('29995c34-bd2a-41ee-af50-b363adf38c1e', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 24, 'A patient undergoes an elective repeat cesarean delivery for a history of two prior cesarean sections, with no other complications this pregnancy. Which of the following best reflects correct coding for the indication?');
VALUES ('29995c34-bd2a-41ee-af50-b363adf38c1e', 'e963cf9d-7a0e-41ba-9595-7d6c6e49d175', 'ICD-10-CM includes a specific code category for pregnancy/delivery management related to a history of prior cesarean delivery — using this specific, clinically accurate code rather than either omitting the indication or defaulting to an unrelated complication code reflects correct obstetric coding specificity.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('3efe8ecb-757a-40f9-832f-1b248a57f15e', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 25, 'Which of the following best describes coding for a newborn who requires respiratory support and is diagnosed with ''transient tachypnea of the newborn'' (TTN)?');
VALUES ('3efe8ecb-757a-40f9-832f-1b248a57f15e', 'bc5ccf13-5fc6-4b7e-87ca-6ffbdd6984ef', 'TTN is a distinct, generally self-limited neonatal respiratory condition with its own specific ICD-10-CM code — conflating it with RDS (a different, more severe condition, often associated with prematurity/surfactant deficiency) would misrepresent the newborn''s actual diagnosis.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('9a2de761-28e5-4cbe-a6bf-78b32c91a7bf', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 26, 'A newborn is diagnosed with neonatal abstinence syndrome (NAS) due to maternal opioid use during pregnancy. Which of the following best reflects correct coding on the newborn''s own record?');
VALUES ('9a2de761-28e5-4cbe-a6bf-78b32c91a7bf', 'cfb64cb8-1b44-40d4-a96c-f4336bd5b523', 'ICD-10-CM includes a specific code for neonatal abstinence syndrome/withdrawal symptoms from maternal drug use, reported on the NEWBORN''s own record (in addition to appropriate maternal substance use disorder coding on the mother''s own record) — reflecting the newborn''s own distinct clinical condition.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('a873844a-e8ea-462c-b35d-e51545e9d27f', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 27, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a mother''s own substance use disorder during pregnancy affects the fetus/newborn (e.g., documented maternal opioid use disorder during pregnancy)?');
VALUES ('a873844a-e8ea-462c-b35d-e51545e9d27f', 'db2ceead-c559-4200-b8bf-06714d66c39d', 'This reinforces that mother and newborn are coded on their own SEPARATE records, each capturing their own respective diagnoses (maternal substance use disorder on the mother''s chart; neonatal effects like NAS on the newborn''s own chart) — not merged or coded exclusively on one record.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('7bee66b9-9eff-4bf4-b19d-7a17ba3a288a', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 28, 'A patient with documented generalized anxiety disorder and panic disorder, both actively being treated with separate medications, would generally be coded using:');
VALUES ('7bee66b9-9eff-4bf4-b19d-7a17ba3a288a', 'bdf8082d-1ec1-45e2-8bb6-c60c21b71d3f', 'Generalized anxiety disorder and panic disorder are distinct, separately classified conditions in ICD-10-CM — both are coded when both are actively addressed/treated, reinforcing that ICD-10-CM doesn''t limit behavioral health coding to a single diagnosis per encounter.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('444f5b7c-94fb-45cb-a665-c81bdf2696c0', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 29, 'Which of the following best describes ICD-10-CM''s classification structure for eating disorders (e.g., anorexia nervosa, bulimia nervosa)?');
VALUES ('444f5b7c-94fb-45cb-a665-c81bdf2696c0', 'a7c58e4a-53d3-4dc5-8746-82f6f07691a9', 'Eating disorders occupy their own distinct ICD-10-CM code category, separate from both general malnutrition codes and mood/anxiety disorder codes — recognizing these as their own clinically and codably distinct entities reflects accurate behavioral-health coding knowledge.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('576e830b-2b6e-42bb-9df4-5a094113a874', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 30, 'A patient with documented post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) related to combat exposure is admitted for an unrelated medical condition. If the PTSD is not addressed/evaluated during this specific admission, correct coding practice generally directs:');
VALUES ('576e830b-2b6e-42bb-9df4-5a094113a874', 'ca3b8564-4be8-47a3-8f58-bf7ab61bd0e4', 'This reinforces that the UHDDS additional-diagnosis test applies uniformly across condition types (including serious chronic behavioral health diagnoses) — clinical significance to the patient generally doesn''t override the encounter-specific relevance test for THIS particular admission''s coding.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('bc54b25a-8897-4396-a95b-1d977e7f082e', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 31, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-CM coding for a documented ''opioid use disorder, in early remission, currently on methadone maintenance therapy''?');
VALUES ('bc54b25a-8897-4396-a95b-1d977e7f082e', 'ffc74fa6-57b5-4f34-9bc5-2ce15f57e1dc', 'ICD-10-CM''s substance use disorder codes capture remission status when documented, and appropriate additional coding conventions exist to reflect medication-assisted treatment context — coding a patient in documented remission on maintenance therapy identically to an active, untreated use disorder would misrepresent their actual clinical status.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('e1308c02-c9c4-4597-bcc7-4b99afe4960b', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 32, 'A patient sustains a traumatic brain injury (TBI) with documented loss of consciousness of a specific duration. Which of the following best reflects the coding relevance of documented LOC duration for TBI coding?');
VALUES ('e1308c02-c9c4-4597-bcc7-4b99afe4960b', 'a558935a-fcd2-4d94-a26d-ea449d5d7f04', 'TBI code selection in ICD-10-CM frequently depends on documented LOC duration/status — this is a real code-selection-determining detail, not merely incidental clinical description, reinforcing the general principle that injury coding often depends on specific documented clinical parameters.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('91728ad3-fa15-4c0a-87f4-0240f8f5051e', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 33, 'Which of the following best describes the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) coding option available in ICD-10-CM for a patient with a traumatic brain injury?');
VALUES ('91728ad3-fa15-4c0a-87f4-0240f8f5051e', '14e78862-e79f-4778-bf25-348b5c035916', 'ICD-10-CM provides specific GCS-component codes that can supplement a TBI (or other applicable) diagnosis when the GCS score is documented — an example of ICD-10-CM capturing clinically granular severity data beyond the base diagnosis code itself.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('2529beb3-8f10-4e9b-a54d-e81588036ea9', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 34, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-PCS coding for a craniotomy performed to evacuate an epidural hematoma?');
VALUES ('2529beb3-8f10-4e9b-a54d-e81588036ea9', 'ac9e2aff-98f8-42f0-878f-5d1c0df7d42c', 'A clotted hematoma (solid matter, not free-flowing fluid) evacuated without a natural/created channel to the outside maps to Extirpation — distinguishing it from Drainage, which is reserved for taking out fluids/gases rather than solid/clotted matter.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('1e4668af-8a41-48ce-9393-728c988b140c', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 35, 'Which of the following best describes the coding treatment of a ''burn'' injury with respect to percentage of total body surface area (TBSA) documentation?');
VALUES ('1e4668af-8a41-48ce-9393-728c988b140c', '1ae6eb04-e8ce-45aa-8db5-f5e716487bfe', 'ICD-10-CM provides a distinct code category specifically for TBSA percentage (and percentage with third-degree involvement) as SUPPLEMENTAL codes to the primary site/degree-specific burn codes — a frequently under-recognized but guideline-relevant burn-coding element.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('4e92f31b-60a7-4919-aad2-e563e89cb32e', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 36, 'Which of the following best describes the correct sequencing principle for multiple burns of different degrees at different sites sustained in the same incident?');
VALUES ('4e92f31b-60a7-4919-aad2-e563e89cb32e', '7d99bef2-d8e4-4648-a33d-60b667539c3d', 'The burn coding guideline directs sequencing the highest-degree (most severe) burn first among multiple sites — a specific, guideline-driven sequencing rule distinct from a generic alphabetical or size-based approach.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('7461c7bd-586d-4088-8f93-0d3ff91a8638', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 37, 'A patient undergoes a bone marrow transplant (hematopoietic stem cell transplant) using cells from a matched, unrelated donor. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation and qualifier concept applies?');
VALUES ('7461c7bd-586d-4088-8f93-0d3ff91a8638', '06f240ba-5a80-405c-ac8f-6aee0a56fb24', 'Hematopoietic stem cell/bone marrow transplantation maps to the Transplantation root operation, with the qualifier reflecting the specific donor-source relationship (allogeneic/related, allogeneic/unrelated, or autologous) — a specific, commonly tested oncology/hematology ICD-10-PCS scenario.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('4070c650-2290-4a5e-82eb-38c2f61c8fb2', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 38, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a patient receiving chemotherapy via a central venous access device (e.g., a port) placed during a prior, separate encounter?');
VALUES ('4070c650-2290-4a5e-82eb-38c2f61c8fb2', 'e9ade9d5-d8e2-449e-b5e4-ccf1dac2aa93', 'This reinforces the earlier chemotherapy-encounter sequencing principle while clarifying that a device placed during a PRIOR, separate encounter isn''t re-coded at each subsequent administration visit — only procedures actually performed during the current encounter are coded for that encounter.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('81d5ac2a-b357-4306-8526-99ba3717f3ac', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 39, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for radiation therapy delivered in multiple fractions (sessions) over several weeks, from an inpatient coding perspective if a patient happens to be hospitalized during ongoing radiation treatment for an unrelated reason?');
VALUES ('81d5ac2a-b357-4306-8526-99ba3717f3ac', '41c466c8-5d9e-44ef-8376-940906722063', 'Radiation oncology procedures actually performed during an inpatient stay (even if for a different underlying reason than the primary admission) are coded per the applicable session-based codes actually delivered — reflecting the general principle that all procedures actually performed during a stay are coded, not just those directly tied to the principal diagnosis.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('1bd59569-fe4d-491c-bc39-2d42df6eb7e8', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 40, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a patient with a known genetic mutation (e.g., BRCA1) who undergoes prophylactic (risk-reducing) bilateral mastectomy with no current cancer diagnosis?');
VALUES ('1bd59569-fe4d-491c-bc39-2d42df6eb7e8', '0ad9e35d-e987-444d-8ddd-1608acd89cfb', 'A prophylactic/risk-reducing procedure is coded using the appropriate risk-factor/genetic-susceptibility code (not an active malignancy code, since no cancer is present) to support medical necessity, alongside the procedure code for the surgery actually performed — a nuanced but real oncology-adjacent coding scenario.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

-- ── Section: Domain 2 — Coding Documentation (20 questions) — Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance. ──

VALUES ('9ac3e01c-8e3a-4709-8609-492049e7794d', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 41, 'A mother''s delivery record documents ''delivered a healthy baby'' with no further detail on single vs. multiple gestation, type of delivery, or outcome anywhere else in the chart. This is best addressed by:');
VALUES ('9ac3e01c-8e3a-4709-8609-492049e7794d', '48bc3967-8456-411c-9081-25860b7bcdc9', 'A vague summary phrase doesn''t supply the specific details needed for accurate obstetric coding — the correct response is reviewing the broader labor/delivery documentation (which often DOES contain the needed detail elsewhere), reserving a query for cases where the full record genuinely doesn''t resolve the ambiguity.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('98a72153-8e62-4845-9a14-cd280578ad7c', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 42, 'A psychiatric admission note documents ''MDD'' without specifying episode type (single/recurrent), severity, or remission status, while a later discharge summary states ''depression, in partial remission, on sertraline.'' The correct documentation-review approach is:');
VALUES ('98a72153-8e62-4845-9a14-cd280578ad7c', 'c789636f-e3ce-4f4e-8513-98814324e45b', 'Reviewing the full course of a stay (not stopping at the first, least detailed note) often resolves apparent specificity gaps — later documentation supplying needed detail should be used, reserving queries for genuine, unresolved ambiguity across the whole record.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('5b4d0e45-51ed-401f-8e4d-b6bac44eeb62', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 43, 'A substance use treatment note documents ''AUD'' (an abbreviation) without specifying severity (mild/moderate/severe) anywhere in the current encounter''s documentation, though a prior treatment episode''s records (available in the same health system) documented ''severe alcohol use disorder'' six months ago. The correct approach is:');
VALUES ('5b4d0e45-51ed-401f-8e4d-b6bac44eeb62', '1d3c293f-5612-4fba-a154-f06c935fa83f', 'Substance use disorder severity is not necessarily static — assuming a prior encounter''s documented severity automatically carries forward to the current encounter risks inaccuracy in either direction; current-encounter clinical documentation (or a query) should establish the CURRENT status.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('decd8bef-97ff-4186-b24c-e092743118db', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 44, 'A trauma patient''s chart documents ''assault'' in the ED triage note, but the police report (scanned into the chart) states the injury was accidental, and no physician note anywhere addresses or resolves this discrepancy. Which best reflects appropriate handling for external cause/intent coding?');
VALUES ('decd8bef-97ff-4186-b24c-e092743118db', '4a665c50-914f-4bc6-9d43-b5e91b1f3f9d', 'A genuine conflict between a triage note and an external document (like a police report) is a real documentation-integrity issue for intent-specific coding — the correct approach is further clinical-documentation review and, if unresolved, a query, not automatically favoring either source without review.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('e59a9387-25d6-4127-b726-2cd9a63ce642', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 45, 'Which best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity consideration when a newborn''s Apgar scores are documented by a nurse, while gestational age is documented by the delivering physician, and NICU admission diagnoses are documented by a neonatologist — three different sources contributing to one newborn''s overall record?');
VALUES ('e59a9387-25d6-4127-b726-2cd9a63ce642', '48563b79-eedd-4c36-843b-dd2010635098', 'Newborn coding legitimately and routinely draws on multiple qualified documentation sources for different data elements (nursing for certain structured measurements like Apgar, physicians for diagnoses) — recognizing this multi-source pattern as normal and appropriate (rather than requiring everything from one single note) reflects accurate real-world newborn documentation review.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('d302f9c9-fa1e-4614-8b9a-48699a40999c', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 46, 'A discharge summary for a patient treated for opioid use disorder documents ''patient doing well on Suboxone'' with no note anywhere specifying current severity or remission status of the underlying use disorder. The correct documentation-review approach is:');
VALUES ('d302f9c9-fa1e-4614-8b9a-48699a40999c', '8ebf17f9-0e94-4edd-b439-6e32a6a69279', 'General positive-progress language doesn''t itself supply the specific severity/remission classification ICD-10-CM''s substance use disorder codes require — this calls for further record review or a query, not an assumption in either direction.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('e75fd8b7-df6f-4941-abb6-cd7c5dee80e8', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 47, 'A trauma surgery operative report documents ''splenic laceration, grade III, repaired'' with the specific repair technique described in detail, but the admission H&P''s mechanism-of-injury section simply states ''MVC'' with no further detail on the patient''s role (driver, passenger, pedestrian) or other circumstances relevant to external cause coding. This gap is best addressed by:');
VALUES ('e75fd8b7-df6f-4941-abb6-cd7c5dee80e8', 'b196932f-76d0-4ae5-a951-c3f8f8871af9', 'A brief H&P notation is rarely the only documentation source for injury circumstances — EMS records, ED triage notes, and other parts of the record often supply the needed external-cause detail, making broader record review (rather than assumption or skipping) the correct first step.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('aa2b0d2b-f2bc-4030-8fde-ad96c6d0e0ab', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 48, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity practice specifically for coding TBSA (total body surface area) percentage in burn cases?');
VALUES ('aa2b0d2b-f2bc-4030-8fde-ad96c6d0e0ab', 'd500c176-4615-4c2d-8067-ac3ab56dafaf', 'TBSA percentage coding should be grounded in actual clinical documentation (a burn diagram or clinical assessment specifically calculating the percentage) — a coder should not estimate or assume this figure without documented clinical support.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('4c1ef0cd-f757-49e9-8000-c1e145757674', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 49, 'A patient''s chart shows an obstetric ultrasound report documenting a specific gestational age at the time of a mid-pregnancy visit, while the physician''s own progress note from the same visit states a different, inconsistent gestational age. Which best reflects the appropriate documentation-review approach?');
VALUES ('4c1ef0cd-f757-49e9-8000-c1e145757674', '4e9b7b2b-b7ea-4031-bdfa-a04a130836d7', 'Gestational age discrepancies between dating methods (LMP vs. ultrasound) are a genuine, recognized clinical documentation nuance requiring review of the full prenatal record rather than an automatic assumption that one source is always correct or an outcome-driven selection of whichever number is more ''favorable.''', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('c2794bf4-527f-4f80-8446-d34c8fdeace3', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 50, 'Which best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity consideration when a patient''s psychiatric admission note and nursing behavioral health assessment describe similar but not identical symptom pictures (e.g., psychiatrist notes ''major depressive episode,'' nursing notes describe symptoms also consistent with an anxiety component not mentioned by the psychiatrist)?');
VALUES ('c2794bf4-527f-4f80-8446-d34c8fdeace3', '4449eff2-d55f-4e8b-973f-740bcc349f79', 'This reinforces the general principle (already established for physical health scenarios) that nursing clinical assessments provide valuable context but don''t themselves establish a distinct diagnosis — applied here to behavioral health, where the same documentation-hierarchy logic applies.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('5354a153-3a4a-4fec-9bde-454598a8a8c2', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 51, 'A patient''s record includes a genetic counseling note referencing ''BRCA1 positive, discussed risk-reducing surgical options,'' but no surgeon''s note anywhere in the CURRENT encounter documents this genetic status as the indication for a subsequently scheduled prophylactic mastectomy. From a documentation-completeness standpoint for the surgical encounter, this is best addressed by:');
VALUES ('5354a153-3a4a-4fec-9bde-454598a8a8c2', 'cccde86e-3875-436c-90ef-199edfc00b39', 'While the genetic counseling documentation is a valid and important part of the overall record, the surgical encounter''s own documentation should support the indication for that specific procedure — reviewing the surgeon''s complete documentation (which may appropriately reference the genetic finding) or querying addresses a genuine gap, rather than assuming an unrelated indication or ignoring relevant prior findings entirely.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('837d3b85-8746-4a70-ad03-70d695c91d5b', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 52, 'Which of the following best describes a documentation-integrity practice specifically relevant to coding hematopoietic stem cell transplant donor-source qualifiers (autologous vs. allogeneic-related vs. allogeneic-unrelated)?');
VALUES ('837d3b85-8746-4a70-ad03-70d695c91d5b', 'c5007627-5c90-489b-871d-f1cd9c033e6d', 'This reinforces the device/qualifier-specificity documentation principle applied to transplant donor-source coding specifically — a real, code-selection-determining detail requiring actual documentation, not assumption based on general facility patterns.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('91e5c9f2-f456-4906-8834-928896931964', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 53, 'A patient''s chart contains a radiation oncology treatment planning note and separate daily treatment delivery logs, with the planning note specifying a total planned number of fractions that doesn''t match the number of delivery-log entries actually present for a given inpatient stay''s radiation sessions. Which best reflects appropriate documentation review here?');
VALUES ('91e5c9f2-f456-4906-8834-928896931964', 'b0e70859-f635-4ec4-bcab-d926fa347208', 'Coding for a specific inpatient stay should reflect fractions ACTUALLY delivered during that stay (per the delivery logs), not the overall treatment plan''s total fraction count, which may span well beyond the current admission — reconciling the two sources ensures the specific encounter''s coding is accurate.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('0f74f01f-7090-4735-b4c0-9e0cf2dcbbbd', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 54, 'A newborn''s chart shows an Apgar score documented by the delivery room nurse at 1 and 5 minutes, but the pediatrician''s admission note to the newborn nursery doesn''t restate these scores or reference them. From a documentation-sufficiency standpoint for coding purposes (where Apgar-related coding may be relevant), this is best characterized as:');
VALUES ('0f74f01f-7090-4735-b4c0-9e0cf2dcbbbd', '621b32f8-6bf5-4bdd-a00b-dbb73f3916b0', 'Apgar scores are a recognized instance of the same documentation-source principle already established — a structured, nursing/delivery-team-documented data element that doesn''t require independent physician restatement to be used in newborn coding.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('f07a2eb8-1c8c-47cb-9a70-91ff1bafb91c', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 55, 'A patient''s chart includes conflicting documentation about whether an injury resulted from a fall (accidental) or was inflicted by another person (assault), with an ED social work note raising a concern about possible non-accidental trauma that no physician note addresses further. Which best reflects the appropriate documentation-integrity response?');
VALUES ('f07a2eb8-1c8c-47cb-9a70-91ff1bafb91c', '549c77ed-8dc1-41c4-9c90-a3f5de531127', 'A social work concern about possible non-accidental trauma is a significant clinical signal (often triggering its own protocol/reporting obligations) but doesn''t itself resolve the intent question for coding — further physician documentation or a query is the appropriate path, not an automatic determination in either direction based on the social work note alone.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('4cba1e4b-cf71-4d2c-9d31-8efe710ea1fc', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 56, 'Which best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity practice when reviewing a patient''s chart for HCC-relevant chronic conditions in the context of a telehealth (virtual) encounter, as distinguished from an in-person visit?');
VALUES ('4cba1e4b-cf71-4d2c-9d31-8efe710ea1fc', 'e26da14b-084a-4cad-bab1-1e088e84bb64', 'Visit modality (telehealth vs. in-person) doesn''t change the underlying documentation-sufficiency standard — a condition still needs to be genuinely evaluated/addressed and documented at that specific encounter (whatever its modality) to support coding, consistent with the general additional-diagnosis and HCC-relevance principles already established.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('4319a455-bd6a-438a-92df-b2bc853baae2', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 57, 'A patient''s chart contains a scanned outside hospital transfer summary referencing ''history of substance use disorder, in remission per patient report,'' with no verification or independent assessment by the receiving facility''s own treating physician. Which best reflects the appropriate documentation-review approach for the RECEIVING facility''s own coding of this encounter?');
VALUES ('4319a455-bd6a-438a-92df-b2bc853baae2', '611983fa-eed3-4f72-adb6-cb118813bec0', 'This reinforces the earlier principle about a treating physician''s own clinical acknowledgment of outside information — an unverified, patient-reported history relayed via an outside summary shouldn''t be adopted for coding purposes without the RECEIVING facility''s own treating physician incorporating it into their own documented clinical assessment.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('80776317-d755-4546-8835-78e1a4af1550', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 58, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate documentation practice for capturing information-blocking-relevant metadata (e.g., timestamps of when clinical notes become available to patients via a portal) from a HIM compliance-monitoring standpoint?');
VALUES ('80776317-d755-4546-8835-78e1a4af1550', 'ad244cc6-0543-43b0-91e9-5cefd29dae01', 'Tracking note-release timing metadata is a practical way to monitor and demonstrate information-blocking compliance (timely patient access) — connecting general HIM recordkeeping/monitoring practice to the broader interoperability compliance obligations discussed elsewhere in this content.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('e998e8ac-581d-4b1f-87a9-0c69a81e56a5', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 59, 'A patient''s substance use disorder treatment record (covered under 42 CFR Part 2) is scanned into the same general EHR system used for the patient''s other, non-Part-2-covered care. Which best reflects an appropriate documentation-system-design consideration here?');
VALUES ('e998e8ac-581d-4b1f-87a9-0c69a81e56a5', '1ae59bc9-7560-4acd-bb2d-6f48f4ce0172', 'Practical compliance with Part 2''s heightened requirements generally requires some system-level ability to identify/segregate covered records so the correct (more restrictive) handling can actually be applied — treating all EHR content identically would risk either over-restricting non-covered records or under-protecting genuinely covered ones.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('122e5d8d-be72-439b-8a2d-541fa0c0c618', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 60, 'Which of the following best describes a documentation-integrity consideration when a patient''s chart shows a completed genetic test result (e.g., BRCA1 positive) filed in the record, but the ordering physician''s own interpretive note discussing clinical significance and next steps is not yet present (pending) at the time coding would otherwise occur for a related encounter?');
VALUES ('122e5d8d-be72-439b-8a2d-541fa0c0c618', 'd7aa4a00-820e-4573-92a9-744333d95709', 'This reinforces the earlier general principle (already applied to lab/imaging findings) that a raw test result isn''t automatically equivalent to a physician''s own diagnostic interpretation — applied here to genetic testing specifically, where the clinical significance interpretation genuinely requires physician judgment beyond the raw result itself.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

-- ── Section: Domain 3 — Provider Queries (10 questions) — Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles). ──

VALUES ('f8dcb29e-9a0a-4189-97d2-1c1c7ad7c0ca', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 61, 'A compliant query regarding unspecified major depressive disorder documentation might appropriately ask the physician to clarify which of the following, among non-leading options?');
VALUES ('f8dcb29e-9a0a-4189-97d2-1c1c7ad7c0ca', '447b691d-815f-4598-8b5a-7838b8ac604c', 'A compliant behavioral-health specificity query presents the genuine clinical dimensions needing clarification (episode pattern, severity, remission) as open, multi-option choices — not a yes/no confirmation of a single suspected answer, and not framed around insurance/coverage considerations.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('a3331e10-aa0a-41a4-9603-db1100baca71', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 62, 'Which best reflects appropriate query practice when clarifying whether a documented injury''s intent was accidental, self-harm, or assault, given the sensitivity of this clinical/legal topic?');
VALUES ('a3331e10-aa0a-41a4-9603-db1100baca71', 'eb562006-a2eb-41d4-ab70-8ae859341b20', 'Sensitive topics like injury intent still follow the same non-leading query-construction principles as any other clinical ambiguity — they aren''t exempt from query practice, but do warrant particularly careful, clinically-grounded wording given the topic''s sensitivity.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('ed202a51-f901-4aa1-a2b5-1cb920b68f14', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 63, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate query approach for clarifying substance use disorder severity when current documentation is ambiguous, given the sensitive nature of behavioral health/substance use information (including special confidentiality protections that may apply under federal law, e.g., 42 CFR Part 2)?');
VALUES ('ed202a51-f901-4aa1-a2b5-1cb920b68f14', '1304a712-7527-4abb-9f2c-fe0483ae2fa5', '42 CFR Part 2''s heightened confidentiality protections govern DISCLOSURE of substance use treatment information to outside parties — they don''t prohibit a treating facility''s own internal coding/CDI query process for accurately documenting/coding that same treatment episode, a nuance worth distinguishing from a blanket ''never query'' assumption.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('7e61e2ea-62a1-4a86-bdbc-fac8ab2ac2a0', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 64, 'A compliant query regarding obstetric documentation might appropriately ask a physician to clarify which of the following, given conflicting gestational age documentation between an early ultrasound and a later progress note?');
VALUES ('7e61e2ea-62a1-4a86-bdbc-fac8ab2ac2a0', '12ac79d8-9f26-485e-929a-7ab71b19be08', 'A compliant query for a clinically genuine dating discrepancy asks the physician for their own clinical determination based on the full available information — framed as an open clinical question, not a preference poll or insurance-driven request.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('9e385410-a836-4a8f-952a-9de06e2edc1b', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 65, 'Which best reflects an appropriate query approach when a patient''s mental status/behavioral health documentation is ambiguous regarding a possible additional diagnosis (e.g., possible co-occurring anxiety alongside a documented depressive disorder), constructed to remain non-leading?');
VALUES ('9e385410-a836-4a8f-952a-9de06e2edc1b', 'b1270397-d362-4dbc-93cc-eb76cc05a9c0', 'This applies the standard non-leading, multi-option query construction principle to a behavioral health scenario — presenting the clinical ambiguity and offering genuine options (not presuming the specific answer) keeps the query compliant.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('2b54c47b-9fcd-42e4-a7ea-8fd68af4ef78', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 66, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate query practice when documentation is ambiguous about donor-source type (autologous vs. allogeneic) for a stem cell transplant procedure?');
VALUES ('2b54c47b-9fcd-42e4-a7ea-8fd68af4ef78', '01038e01-5530-4177-a1e8-e5f1742b1e00', 'Donor-source type is a genuine, physician/procedure-team-knowable clinical fact appropriate for a straightforward, non-leading query when documentation doesn''t clearly specify it — not an administrative detail to skip, nor something to assume by default frequency.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('2a197311-93ed-4c3e-bf48-047fb789235e', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 67, 'Which best describes appropriate query construction when clarifying whether an injury''s external cause reflects intentional self-harm, given the clinical sensitivity and potential safety-planning implications of the answer?');
VALUES ('2a197311-93ed-4c3e-bf48-047fb789235e', '39038fdf-e4fa-4321-b7d6-9a38f3e43018', 'Given both the coding-accuracy need and the clinical safety-planning implications of an accurate self-harm determination, a well-constructed non-leading query remains the appropriate path — sensitivity argues for careful, professional framing, not avoidance of the query altogether.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('5ede3762-cb0a-47a2-9197-c8bda40ccd8b', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 68, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate query practice regarding whether a documented pediatric/newborn condition (e.g., a specific congenital anomaly) was confirmed via genetic/diagnostic testing versus clinically suspected but not yet confirmed?');
VALUES ('5ede3762-cb0a-47a2-9197-c8bda40ccd8b', 'f93899e7-8967-4dd5-803a-5f8e1d7c9226', 'Diagnostic certainty (confirmed vs. suspected) genuinely affects coding treatment, including whether the inpatient uncertain-diagnosis guideline properly applies — a non-leading clarifying query is appropriate when the record doesn''t make this clear, consistent with the general specificity-query principles already established.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('25065821-8ae4-4852-a72e-beec58ab9e83', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 69, 'Which best describes an appropriate response when a query regarding TBSA (total body surface area) burn percentage receives a physician response citing a specific percentage, but that percentage appears inconsistent with the documented individual body-site burn descriptions elsewhere in the chart?');
VALUES ('25065821-8ae4-4852-a72e-beec58ab9e83', '312c813f-bbb6-4703-bfd4-d4c63a264b5c', 'Receiving a query response doesn''t eliminate the coder''s general documentation-review responsibility — an apparent internal inconsistency between the query response and other chart documentation is worth a follow-up clarification, rather than either blindly accepting the response or independently estimating a figure the coder isn''t clinically positioned to determine themselves.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('49908479-6076-4774-b696-306e119089ca', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 70, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate query-compliance consideration specific to querying about information that might be subject to 42 CFR Part 2 protections (e.g., clarifying details of a substance use disorder diagnosis)?');
VALUES ('49908479-6076-4774-b696-306e119089ca', '7d838118-8f73-4cf6-8cc5-d959104de3d1', 'This reinforces the earlier distinction between Part 2''s third-party disclosure restrictions and a treating facility''s own internal query/coding process for that same treatment episode — the query itself follows standard non-leading compliance principles, since it isn''t a disclosure to an outside party.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

-- ── Section: Domain 4 — Regulatory Compliance (20 questions) — Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance. ──

VALUES ('b1a84b25-85b4-4af9-a9f3-8d8af3b1450f', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 71, 'The federal Anti-Kickback Statute (AKS) is best described as:');
VALUES ('b1a84b25-85b4-4af9-a9f3-8d8af3b1450f', '831e6bec-6b02-43cb-b257-a500968f1624', 'AKS is a criminal fraud/abuse statute targeting improper remuneration tied to federal healthcare program referrals — a distinct legal framework from HIPAA privacy/security law, though both fall under the broader healthcare compliance umbrella relevant to Domain 4.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('0ef59149-6237-40fd-932d-e1915d3c1a5c', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 72, 'The Stark Law (Physician Self-Referral Law) is best described as:');
VALUES ('0ef59149-6237-40fd-932d-e1915d3c1a5c', '0d155b30-8dd0-4fa4-b88e-21d589a587c8', 'Stark Law is a distinct, strict-liability self-referral prohibition (no intent requirement, unlike AKS) targeting physician financial relationships tied to designated health service referrals — understanding it as legally distinct from AKS (different elements, different exceptions/safe harbors) is a relevant compliance-literacy point.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('ae8ac1a9-d35d-47c8-a8f2-d17d0d4bcfa4', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 73, 'Which of the following best describes why HIM/coding compliance knowledge sometimes intersects with AKS/Stark Law considerations, even though coders don''t typically negotiate physician contracts?');
VALUES ('ae8ac1a9-d35d-47c8-a8f2-d17d0d4bcfa4', '8aab5997-d41b-48ed-a97f-e72d8990430e', 'While coders don''t negotiate physician arrangements, coded/billing data can become relevant evidence in AKS/Stark-related investigations (e.g., unusual referral-linked utilization patterns) — general compliance-program literacy across these adjacent fraud/abuse frameworks is a reasonable part of a well-rounded HIM/coding compliance foundation.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('1d126bbe-53cb-4721-a730-d18500a6f42d', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 74, 'The 21st Century Cures Act''s ''information blocking'' provisions generally prohibit which of the following, subject to defined exceptions?');
VALUES ('1d126bbe-53cb-4721-a730-d18500a6f42d', '281dedc7-6f1b-4a40-bda2-a1aff8856aae', 'Information blocking provisions target unreasonable interference with EHI access/exchange/use by regulated actors (providers, health IT developers, HIEs/HINs), subject to specific, defined exceptions (e.g., legitimate privacy, security, or infeasibility grounds) — not a blanket ''must share everything publicly'' rule.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('08a47200-5bc1-4d89-929d-986c895f8675', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 75, 'Which of the following best describes a practical HIM/coding-adjacent consideration relevant to information-blocking compliance (e.g., regarding patient access to their own health information, including certain clinical notes)?');
VALUES ('08a47200-5bc1-4d89-929d-986c895f8675', '34f9baa8-6fad-4939-bbf4-d03206896e83', 'Information-blocking compliance has direct, practical relevance to HIM release-of-information and patient-portal note-access practices — the expectation is timely, non-obstructed patient access to their EHI (including many clinical notes) absent a genuinely applicable exception, not blanket withholding or complete disregard of the rule.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('1f2d620e-0aa0-4ba1-8e4b-72602029d677', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 76, 'Which of the following best describes a legitimate, recognized exception under the information-blocking regulations (as distinguished from an impermissible, self-serving reason to withhold information)?');
VALUES ('1f2d620e-0aa0-4ba1-8e4b-72602029d677', 'f4169108-f96b-4dcf-9070-dbb128631b6c', 'Information-blocking exceptions are narrowly and specifically defined (harm prevention, privacy, security, and several other defined categories) with actual conditions that must be met — a generic administrative-convenience or cost-based justification does not qualify as a legitimate exception.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('bfbf5ba0-1ce1-4f94-bc16-3e85f6eddb25', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 77, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance relevance of behavioral health/substance use disorder documentation being subject to BOTH HIPAA AND 42 CFR Part 2, when the two frameworks'' requirements differ?');
VALUES ('bfbf5ba0-1ce1-4f94-bc16-3e85f6eddb25', 'ca1198b4-07bc-4ddb-832a-7c82cae1621f', '42 CFR Part 2 layers additional, generally more restrictive consent/disclosure requirements on top of HIPAA for covered substance use disorder treatment records — HIM staff need dual-framework awareness (applying the more protective standard where they diverge), not treating the two as interchangeable or assuming one fully displaces the other.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('315d8f28-dcb4-4339-bb4b-35d5cedd8cab', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 78, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason for a facility to have clear internal policy distinguishing which specific data elements/notes are subject to 42 CFR Part 2''s heightened protections versus standard HIPAA protections?');
VALUES ('315d8f28-dcb4-4339-bb4b-35d5cedd8cab', 'c54043fe-6c1c-4588-b32a-02f1e1b0800d', 'Since 42 CFR Part 2 has its own specific coverage criteria (not simply ''any behavioral health record''), clear internal policy helps avoid both under-protecting genuinely covered records and over-restricting records that don''t actually meet the specific coverage definition — a practical, real compliance-program design consideration.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('bd3b0d7b-a2b9-449d-b1c6-f15a0cf5aa51', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 79, 'Which of the following best describes the difference between the Anti-Kickback Statute''s intent requirement and the Stark Law''s strict-liability structure?');
VALUES ('bd3b0d7b-a2b9-449d-b1c6-f15a0cf5aa51', '74070a84-4a14-418e-b53e-93c5e2915c4a', 'This is one of the most fundamental AKS-vs-Stark distinctions: AKS generally requires proof of intent (a criminal statute), while Stark is strict liability (a civil statute) — a technical Stark violation can occur without any intent to violate the law, unlike AKS.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('a88d9356-821d-4ab6-a518-5d23015d7959', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 80, 'Which of the following best describes a ''safe harbor'' under the Anti-Kickback Statute?');
VALUES ('a88d9356-821d-4ab6-a518-5d23015d7959', 'ba6a8d1c-162a-4aa7-928d-2070d590f1d9', 'AKS safe harbors provide specific structural protection when fully satisfied — failing to fit a safe harbor doesn''t automatically mean illegality, but does mean the arrangement loses that particular protection and requires broader compliance risk analysis (a nuance distinct from Stark Law''s own separate exception structure).', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('0f986775-b73e-4601-bf32-8dd764fd9f51', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 81, 'Which of the following best describes why understanding AKS/Stark basics is relevant to a compliance officer overseeing an organization''s overall coding/billing compliance program, even though these laws are primarily about referral relationships rather than code selection itself?');
VALUES ('0f986775-b73e-4601-bf32-8dd764fd9f51', 'b2164efc-a14e-4b79-b2ac-85945a768b83', 'A comprehensive compliance program typically spans the full range of relevant fraud/abuse exposure, and coding/billing patterns can intersect with referral-relationship risk areas — baseline cross-framework literacy (not deep legal expertise) supports a well-rounded compliance function.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('e5baca52-4cf3-4478-b118-9559b7c88d08', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 82, 'Which of the following best describes a legitimate Stark Law exception (as distinguished from a prohibited self-referral arrangement)?');
VALUES ('e5baca52-4cf3-4478-b118-9559b7c88d08', 'e654c736-c8ba-4e93-aefd-30482c46a115', 'Stark Law''s exceptions (distinct from AKS''s safe harbors, though addressing similar underlying concerns) permit certain defined financial relationships when their specific structural conditions are fully met — not a blanket exemption for any physician-entity financial relationship.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('c7621ac6-b66e-4802-a96c-50ecf7148b6a', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 83, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance principle behind a hospital''s compliance program including specific policies addressing physician compensation arrangements (e.g., ensuring compensation reflects fair market value and doesn''t vary based on referral volume), even though HIM/coding staff don''t set physician compensation?');
VALUES ('c7621ac6-b66e-4802-a96c-50ecf7148b6a', '4ab78a5c-a536-4a83-b20f-ac91bb21a6e6', 'Compensation tied to referral volume (rather than fair-market-value for actual work) is the classic AKS/Stark risk pattern — while not a HIM/coding-specific function, understanding why this matters supports the broader organizational compliance culture that coding/HIM compliance operates within.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('652ba393-207a-424e-807a-3f3486270944', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 84, 'Which of the following best describes the general relationship between the False Claims Act and AKS/Stark Law violations?');
VALUES ('652ba393-207a-424e-807a-3f3486270944', 'c2a3ddfb-d0d6-4fbd-90ba-ef156fb5267c', 'This is a well-established compliance-enforcement pattern: claims arising from underlying AKS/Stark violations can independently trigger False Claims Act liability, since the claim itself may be considered ''false'' due to the tainted referral relationship — compounding exposure beyond the AKS/Stark violation alone, and reinforcing the earlier False Claims Act ''knowing'' standard discussion in a new context.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('9a762d42-f691-4a54-884e-2a745b58c18f', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 85, 'Which of the following best describes the general concept of ''actual, constructive, or reasonable knowledge'' as it can relate to information-blocking liability for a regulated actor?');
VALUES ('9a762d42-f691-4a54-884e-2a745b58c18f', '7880af15-9aa0-40a6-938b-3beaa17c6456', 'Information-blocking analysis generally involves a knowledge-based standard (varying somewhat by actor type) rather than requiring proof of malicious intent — a practice can raise information-blocking concerns based on its actual effect and the actor''s knowledge, not only where deliberate harm was intended.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('bd8add64-c260-4481-8630-bd1083310098', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 86, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable compliance-program response when a facility identifies that its patient portal''s note-release timing has been inconsistent with information-blocking expectations for a subset of behavioral health notes (perhaps due to an overly broad system configuration treating all behavioral health notes as automatically exempt)?');
VALUES ('bd8add64-c260-4481-8630-bd1083310098', '630fbd22-fd32-46fe-a2e2-c91f5afa17be', 'This applies the broader ''exceptions require actual case-specific conditions, not blanket assumptions'' compliance principle to a concrete patient-portal/behavioral-health scenario — an overly broad categorical exemption (treating an entire note type as automatically exempt) is itself a compliance gap requiring correction to genuinely case-specific application.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('f23b533c-5abf-440e-a92e-663e93f5e3e8', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 87, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason a facility should maintain documentation of its information-blocking exception determinations (e.g., specific instances where a harm-prevention or privacy exception was actually applied to withhold information)?');
VALUES ('f23b533c-5abf-440e-a92e-663e93f5e3e8', 'cc549e5c-1d80-4524-8090-93d61174f98e', 'Documenting specific exception determinations (why an exception was actually, genuinely applied in a given instance) supports demonstrating good-faith, case-specific application — mirroring the broader compliance-program documentation principle already established for other compliance activities (training, auditing, corrective action).', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('198cfbdc-aaab-48dc-a8af-c9fab9be60ef', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 88, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance relevance of ''actor category'' (e.g., healthcare provider vs. health IT developer vs. health information network/exchange) under the information-blocking regulatory framework?');
VALUES ('198cfbdc-aaab-48dc-a8af-c9fab9be60ef', 'd2cc3b69-654f-4af5-b3cc-3ca183f3f3e0', 'Information-blocking requirements/exceptions can have actor-category-specific nuances — understanding which category(ies) apply to a given organization''s actual functions is a practical compliance-literacy point, rather than assuming an undifferentiated single rule set applies identically to every type of regulated actor.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('699a8783-0bf7-46d1-adef-044b58e9ce1f', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 89, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason for a facility''s legal/compliance team (not just clinical staff) to be involved in determining whether a specific 42 CFR Part 2 disclosure request may be honored?');
VALUES ('699a8783-0bf7-46d1-adef-044b58e9ce1f', '2a2de7a1-d293-4d2e-b7c6-628e5031398a', 'Part 2''s heightened, technically specific consent/redisclosure requirements benefit from legal/compliance review alongside clinical input, given their complexity relative to standard HIPAA analysis — reinforcing the broader theme that specialized regulatory frameworks (Part 2, information-blocking, AKS/Stark) each warrant informed, coordinated compliance attention rather than being left to a single department in isolation.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

VALUES ('b23c5974-1fc7-47f1-8779-3941584ec5f8', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 90, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance principle behind a facility ensuring its physician recruitment/employment agreements are reviewed by legal/compliance staff for Stark Law and AKS considerations before execution, rather than relying solely on department-level negotiation?');
VALUES ('b23c5974-1fc7-47f1-8779-3941584ec5f8', '50b9724c-a018-47b1-8144-f3c207a84a00', 'Given that compensation and referral-relationship terms are precisely what Stark/AKS regulate, dedicated legal/compliance review of physician agreements before execution is a standard, recognized risk-mitigation practice — reinforcing why organizational awareness of these frameworks (even outside HIM/coding specifically) supports a coherent overall compliance program.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, Anti-Kickback Statute/Stark Law basics, and 21st Century Cures Act information-blocking guidance.');

-- ── Section: Domain 5 — Information Technologies (10 questions) — Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline covering information-blocking/data-sharing rules and patient access APIs. ──

VALUES ('43737966-8f77-40be-a036-1e9bf49fe9e6', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 91, 'Which of the following best describes a ''patient access API,'' as required under current interoperability/information-blocking regulatory framework?');
VALUES ('43737966-8f77-40be-a036-1e9bf49fe9e6', '05cabc1e-1d17-4105-9c65-c13e559aabff', 'Patient access APIs are specifically designed to let patients access their own health data via apps of their choosing, generally built on standardized formats like FHIR — a concrete implementation of the broader interoperability/information-blocking policy framework discussed in Domain 4.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering information-blocking/data-sharing rules and patient access APIs.');

VALUES ('9986bb10-067c-4e71-b614-7de1c3eea6cc', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 92, 'Which of the following best describes the relevance of standardized data formats (like FHIR) to patient access API requirements, as distinguished from a facility simply providing patients a PDF export of their record?');
VALUES ('9986bb10-067c-4e71-b614-7de1c3eea6cc', '841c5a5e-0475-4986-8b16-f3dd2cf1a3c2', 'The core value of standardized, structured API-based data (versus a static document export) is enabling programmatic use by patient-chosen applications — a PDF is human-readable but not machine-actionable in the same way, which is precisely why the regulatory framework specifies standardized data formats rather than treating any document export as equivalent.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering information-blocking/data-sharing rules and patient access APIs.');

VALUES ('8d6d5a8d-6973-4e4e-a3b6-ef62b4a504f8', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 93, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable HIM/IT governance role in supporting information-blocking/interoperability compliance, beyond pure technical API implementation (an IT function)?');
VALUES ('8d6d5a8d-6973-4e4e-a3b6-ef62b4a504f8', '039da82c-fe4a-4100-88d9-c7ab356d889d', 'Interoperability/information-blocking compliance is a cross-functional effort — HIM contributes essential domain expertise (legitimate exceptions, release-of-information practice, patient-access policy) that complements, rather than being replaced by, the technical API implementation work handled by IT.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering information-blocking/data-sharing rules and patient access APIs.');

VALUES ('5f86a2e5-113d-4149-b131-128e3546e9ba', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 94, 'Which of the following best describes a practical reason HIM/coding leadership should stay informed about evolving interoperability/information-blocking regulatory requirements over time (rather than treating initial compliance as a one-time achievement)?');
VALUES ('5f86a2e5-113d-4149-b131-128e3546e9ba', 'e2f35601-9679-408c-a1c8-b81474b22826', 'Like annual code-set updates and evolving payer policy, interoperability/information-blocking requirements have continued to evolve over time — treating initial implementation as a permanent, one-time achievement rather than an area requiring ongoing monitoring mirrors the same complacency risk already discussed for other regulatory/technology areas in this exam content.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering information-blocking/data-sharing rules and patient access APIs.');

VALUES ('c61e2166-7507-4c49-96f4-767c09787f70', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 95, 'Which of the following best describes a ''health information network'' (HIN) or ''health information exchange'' (HIE), as a distinct actor category from an individual provider organization?');
VALUES ('c61e2166-7507-4c49-96f4-767c09787f70', 'bbde88bd-644f-4c1e-8bc7-3899bdfc9b63', 'HIEs/HINs serve as connective infrastructure enabling exchange AMONG multiple organizations — distinct from, and not a replacement for, an individual provider''s own internal EHR system and its own separate HIPAA obligations.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering information-blocking/data-sharing rules and patient access APIs.');

VALUES ('e066fda8-dff7-428d-8385-54a03f146c52', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 96, 'Which of the following best describes a practical reason HIM professionals should understand basic API/interoperability terminology (even without being software developers themselves), given the discussion of patient access APIs and FHIR resources?');
VALUES ('e066fda8-dff7-428d-8385-54a03f146c52', 'aaafec31-22a3-47ab-a829-096bd5e26b66', 'Foundational conceptual literacy (not coding skill) enables HIM professionals to meaningfully participate in interoperability-related compliance and policy discussions — a practical, achievable competency level distinct from software development expertise.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering information-blocking/data-sharing rules and patient access APIs.');

VALUES ('5455fe1a-6715-41b2-aaea-c54591e3ba2b', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 97, 'Which of the following best describes the relevance of ''USCDI'' (United States Core Data for Interoperability) to patient access API and information-blocking requirements?');
VALUES ('5455fe1a-6715-41b2-aaea-c54591e3ba2b', 'ed67662e-dbb0-46a6-b0ad-cf8d2a0bdbd7', 'USCDI establishes the baseline data classes/elements expected to be interoperably available (feeding into patient access API scope and information-blocking expectations), and has expanded in scope over successive versions — a foundational interoperability-standards concept connecting Domain 4 and Domain 5 content.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering information-blocking/data-sharing rules and patient access APIs.');

VALUES ('ed1a121f-263c-4c2d-968e-cca16c9aa3a8', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 98, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable way to evaluate a health IT vendor''s interoperability/information-blocking-compliance posture during a procurement/vendor-selection process?');
VALUES ('ed1a121f-263c-4c2d-968e-cca16c9aa3a8', 'fee6a3d4-c62a-477c-83c3-bb4cbef1c0a8', 'A thorough procurement process reasonably includes evaluating a health IT vendor''s certification status, interoperability-standard support, and information-blocking compliance posture — since the purchasing organization''s own compliance exposure can be affected by the systems/vendors it selects, not solely the vendor''s independent legal responsibility.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering information-blocking/data-sharing rules and patient access APIs.');

VALUES ('aed35226-a850-44f4-bf3b-e989b08bc88a', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 99, 'Which of the following best describes why ''legacy system'' interoperability challenges (discussed earlier regarding data migration) are particularly relevant to information-blocking compliance specifically, beyond the general data-integrity concern already covered?');
VALUES ('aed35226-a850-44f4-bf3b-e989b08bc88a', 'e124563f-ddc0-44e5-8869-e0a012a21062', 'This connects the earlier legacy-system data-integrity discussion to the information-blocking compliance-exception framework — genuine technical infeasibility with legacy infrastructure may be relevant to an infeasibility-based exception analysis, though this requires actual evaluation, not an automatic blanket assumption of exemption.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering information-blocking/data-sharing rules and patient access APIs.');

VALUES ('d19a9160-c944-480a-b3c9-d15a9f185b93', 'dc28af36-0d99-45ad-a0b9-0c8d93aa918e', 100, 'Which of the following best summarizes the overall Domain 5 theme connecting EHR/CAC technology, interoperability standards (HL7/FHIR), cybersecurity, and information-blocking/patient-access requirements?');
VALUES ('d19a9160-c944-480a-b3c9-d15a9f185b93', '39057795-9224-4784-8eac-0f6cd13e16cc', 'This is a legitimate high-level synthesis: modern HIM/coding technology competency has broadened beyond just coding-support tools to encompass how data moves, is protected, and is made accessible — genuinely connected facets of Domain 5''s information technology competency area, not siloed or interchangeable concepts.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering information-blocking/data-sharing rules and patient access APIs.');
